  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The paper is entirely theoretical and does not provide empirical measurements, spatial data, or quantitative thresholds for the proposed principles.",
      "what": "The paper develops a theoretical framework for transport justice by integrating relational egalitarianism into the concept of accessibility sufficiency. It introduces a relational sufficiency threshold alongside a limitarian principle to constrain how socio-economic privilege is converted into durable accessibility advantages.",
      "why": "It shifts the focus of transport justice from merely preventing basic participation poverty to addressing how accessibility disparities above the poverty line reinforce social hierarchies and second-class status."
    }
